course: dictionary
lesson: 14641
title: Words of order: precede
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Precede means to come or go before something in time, order or place. Picture a line of children: the child at the front precedes the child behind them.
explain: Precede is a formal word, so you will often meet it in books, maths and exam questions. Look for clue words like «preceded by», «the preceding page» and «the events that preceded the war». Each one tells you what came first.
explain: To check a meaning, swap in a simple word such as «came before». If «The storm came before the rainbow» still makes sense, you have understood precede.
fact: Precede is a verb: precede, precedes, preceded, preceding.
fact: «Precede» means to come or go before something in time, order or place.
fact: «Preceding» means coming just before, as in «the preceding page».
fact: In maths, 4 precedes 5 on a number line, and 9 is the number that precedes 10.
fact: In grammar, an article such as «the» precedes a noun, as in «the dog».
exercise: «The storm preceded the rainbow.» What does preceded mean here? | Came after | Looked like | *Came before | Was bigger than
exercise: On a number line, which number precedes 10? | 100 | *9 | 11 | 0
exercise: «Read the preceding page before you answer.» What does preceding mean? | The last page in the book | Written in pencil | Coming just after | *Coming just before
test: «The rain preceded the flood.» Which came first? | The flood | Neither of them | They happened at the same time | *The rain
test: Which word means to come before something? | *Precede | Finish | Follow | Repeat
exam: Which sentence uses precede correctly? | Precede breakfast lunch every day. | Breakfast precede lunch every day. | *Breakfast precedes lunch every day. | Lunch precedes breakfast every day.
